"Arendt grew up in Königsberg, Kants city, in a secular Jewish family. Her father died when she was seven. She read Kant at fourteen, and at eighteen went to Marburg to study with Martin Heidegger, the most exciting philosopher in Germany, with whom she had a love affair. She moved to Heidelberg and wrote her doctoral thesis under Karl Jaspers on the idea of love in Augustine. Jaspers remained her teacher and friend for life.": "929de54c47f8",
"Arendts portrait of Eichmann as a man without motives beyond his career, who never realized what he was doing, was attacked at once. Her friend Gershom Scholem wrote that she lacked love for the Jewish people and that the phrase about banality was a slogan. The more damaging objection came later and from the archives. Recordings that Eichmann made in Argentina in the 1950s, studied closely by the historian Bettina Stangneth, show a committed and boastful antisemite who was proud of his work and regretted that it had not been completed. On this evidence the man in the glass booth in Jerusalem was acting a part, and Arendt was taken in by the performance.": "24f084ebfec4",
"Her defenders grant the facts about Eichmann and hold that the thesis survives them. Arendt did not say that Eichmann was innocent, ordinary, or without convictions. She held that he should hang. Her claim was that great evil does not require demonic depth. It can be done by people who have stopped examining what they say and do, and who take their words ready-made from those around them. A fanatic who speaks in slogans fits that description as well as a clerk does. She may have been wrong about the man and right about the phenomenon, and the experiments of Milgram and others in the following years gave it uncomfortable support.": "b7c3dd8b755e",
"In 1933 Heidegger joined the Nazi party and Arendt began collecting evidence of antisemitic propaganda for a Zionist organization. She was arrested by the Gestapo, held for eight days, and released by an officer she managed to charm. She left Germany without papers and reached Paris, where she worked for seven years for an agency that sent Jewish children to Palestine. She said later that what shocked her in 1933 was how her friends among the intellectuals fell into line. The conduct of her enemies had shocked her less. From then on she distrusted the life of the mind when it is cut off from the world.": "02f1ed7c20e5",
"In 1940 the French interned her as an enemy alien in the camp at Gurs. She walked out during the confusion of the French defeat, found her husband Heinrich Blücher by chance, and with him obtained visas for the United States. Their friend Walter Benjamin gave them a manuscript to carry and killed himself at the Spanish border a few weeks later. They reached New York in May 1941 with very little money and no English.": "9c61430a89c3",
"In 1957 nine Black students entered a white high school in Little Rock, Arkansas, under the protection of federal troops, through crowds of screaming adults. Arendt saw a photograph of one of the girls and wrote an essay arguing that the policy was mistaken. Children, she said, were being sent to fight a political battle that adults had failed to win, and schools belong to the social sphere, where people may choose their company, and not to the political sphere, where equality must be absolute. She thought the first target should have been the laws against marriage between the races.": "3645456f2edf",
"On birth the two stand far apart, and the contrast is instructive. In the Buddhas teaching birth is a link in the chain that leads to aging, death, and sorrow, and the path aims at the end of being born. For Arendt birth is the source of everything new and the reason that the world can be renewed. The difference is less a disagreement about one fact than a difference in what is being looked at. The Buddha is speaking of the round of craving that drives a being from life to life. Arendt is speaking of the appearance of a new person among others, in a shared world. A Mahāyāna Buddhist can accept her point without strain, since the bodhisattva chooses to be born again and again for the sake of that shared world.": "2d1143fba172",
"Philosophers since Plato had taken death as the fact that defines human life. Arendt chose the other end. Every human being arrives as someone who was not there before and who may do what no one expected. She called this condition natality, and she held that it is the root of the human capacity to act, which for her means to start something new among other people. The term she borrowed from her old study of Augustine, who wrote that man was created so that there might be a beginning. Because new people keep arriving, no state of the world is final, and that is the ground of what hope there is in politics.": "105b8f4f4d5c",
"She learned the language, wrote for the émigré press, and worked for an organization that recovered Jewish cultural property looted in Europe. In 1951 she published The Origins of Totalitarianism, which argued that Nazism and Stalinism were a new form of government that worked by isolating people and destroying their ability to tell fact from fiction. At its center is the experience she had lived. A person who has lost a country, she wrote, has lost what she called the right to have rights, since rights are in practice guaranteed only to members of some political community. She became an American citizen in the same year.": "369890d04570",
"She taught at Chicago and then at the New School in New York, and never held a chair of philosophy; she preferred to be called a political theorist. Her last years went to a study of the three activities of the mind, thinking, willing, and judging. She had finished the first two when she died of a heart attack in December 1975, while entertaining friends. The first page of the third part was found in her typewriter with only its title and two epigraphs.": "99cdee23b8f9",
"The Human Condition, in 1958, gave her account of what political life is for, and made her reputation as a thinker. In 1961 she went to Jerusalem for The New Yorker to report on the trial of Adolf Eichmann, who had organized the transport of Jews to the death camps. She expected a monster and described a functionary who spoke in stock phrases and seemed unable to think from anyone elses point of view. Her report, with its subtitle on the banality of evil and its harsh pages on the Jewish councils that had dealt with the Nazis, cost her many friends. She did not withdraw it.": "8fcd67130ab1",
"The essay was a serious error, and it came from her own best ideas. Her concern to shield children from the adult world was the concern of her lecture on education. Her distinction between the social and the political led her to treat a system of public humiliation as a matter of private preference. The novelist Ralph Ellison replied that she had no understanding of what Black parents were doing when they sent their children through those lines. They were teaching them, at a cost they knew, what it would take to live as free people in that country. Arendt wrote to Ellison that he was right, and that she had not grasped the ideal of sacrifice involved. A thinkers distinctions are instruments. This episode shows what happens when they are applied from a distance, without asking the people concerned.": "82cb99b31f2a",
"The idea has a second half that concerns the old. The newcomers arrive in a world they did not make, and it must be in a fit state to receive them. Arendt distinguished the earth, which is given, from the world, which is the human-made home of buildings, institutions, stories, and laws that outlasts each of us. To care for the world is to keep that home standing for people we will not meet. In her lecture on education she drew the conclusion for teachers. Education is where we decide whether we love the world enough to take responsibility for it, and whether we love our children enough to prepare them for the task of renewing it. The teachers authority, she said, rests on being able to say to the child: this is our world.": "3c429e8c9de3",
"They come close on the nature of action. Arendt observed that a deed, once done, cannot be undone, and that its consequences run on without limit and beyond anyones foresight. This is very nearly a description of karma. She proposed two remedies that lie within human power. Against irreversibility there is forgiveness, which releases a person from what he has done so that he can begin again. Against unpredictability there is the promise, which sets up islands of reliability in an uncertain future. Buddhist practice has both, as repentance and as the vow. Her account of Eichmanns fault, a failure to stop and think about what one is doing, is close to what the Buddha called heedlessness, which he named as the path to death, with heedfulness as the path to the deathless.": "563febdf1e57",
